A widely held thesis about holding gold is that it protects us when inflation is high.
The period from 2021-2023 saw CPI rise as high as 8.98%. 

"relatively stable inflation expectations
limit its appeal as an inflation hedge" (cite the report)
The question is: does gold do better than usual when inflation is high? 

holding gold as insurance against a second inflation wave is betting on this, and it rests on 29 months.

D is gold's average return in the 10% of months with the highest CPI prints minus that in
the remaining 253 months. 

$$
D = \bar{r}^{\,\text{gold}}_{\text{flagged}} - \bar{r}^{\,\text{gold}}_{\text{other}}
$$
The data showed that gold returned +0.08% in those months with highest inflation and +1.05% in the others, so
D = −0.97 percentage points per month. 

The null hypothesis H₀: D = 0. This means that high-inflation month is no different for gold than any other month. We are comparing with other months rather than zero because it allows us to see
how gold performs relative to normal, not relative to its own baseline. The alternative hypothesis is then H1: D > 0,
which means gold does outperform when inflation is high.


## Resample to test -0.97

Since we only have one history with relatively few data points, we couldn't know 
whether -0.97 is due to chance or not. Thus, we use bootstrap to randomly select
measurements from the original sample, allowing the same measurement to be selected
multiple times. For each bootstrap sample, we calculate the D, and we repeat the process
many times to get the spread of Ds. 

<iframe src="fig1_bootstrap.html" width="100%" height="560" style="border:none;" loading="lazy" title="Figure 1: bootstrap distribution of D"></iframe>

*Figure 1. Bootstrap distribution of D, gold's return in the flagged months minus its return in other months.*

The histogram shows the distribution of D when we resample by 100, 500, 1000, 5000
and 10000 times. The histogram starts being ragged, and gradually smooth out as we increase
the number of resamples. Resampling many times allows us to get a more reliable
distribution. 
At 10000 times, the 95% interval (shaded band) sits
between −2.8 and +0.9 pp. It's noteworthy that the 95% interval
straddles zero, so we cannot reject the null hypothesis. 
This indicates that gold's high-inflation months are not
distinguishable from its ordinary ones. And if anything, it actually leans
towards the negative direction, with D sitting close to -1pp. 

The figure'd dropdowns also test three other definitions of inflation, including the 12-month rate, and months when expected inflation rose or fell most. We will return to those at the end.


## Simulation to test the 95% bootstrap interval

We use simulation to check whether the interval from Figure 1 actually catches the
true D 95% of the time. We treat our 282 months as
the whole population, so the true D is −0.97. Then, we draw a fresh 24-year history from it, build a 95% bootstrap interval from that history alone, and repeat 1,000 times. 
Each line on the left panel is one simulated history's interval, with a dot at that history's own estimate. The lines are sorted by the
estimate, so that the estimates that miss the truth (rust intervals) sit at the two
ends. The result on the right panel shows that using 24 years of data, 93% of the 1,000 cover the true D, which is close to
95%. We could also see how different years of data change the share the selecting from the dropdown menu. The right panel plots the share of the simulations that cover the truth using 
different windows. Five years of data has the lowest share, but from 10 years on it is
93–94%. As such, an interval built from a short history is less confident that it
reports. 

<iframe src="fig2_coverage.html" width="100%" height="660" style="border:none;" loading="lazy" title="Figure 2: simulated confidence intervals against the true D, and coverage by years of data"></iframe>

*Figure 2. Left: 100 of the 1,000 simulated histories, each drawn from the 282 months with replacement, sorted by the
history's own estimate of D; Right: the
share of all 1,000 intervals that contain the true D, by years of history, against the nominal level.*

## Null distribution and the test

If H₀ is true, high inflation is just a label in 29 months of the sample, and it should not matter which 29. To test whether this is true or not, we shuffle the labels, pick 29 months at random and call them
the inflation months, then compute D, for a total of 10000 times. 
The grayish brown histogram is what D
looks like when nothing is going on. It is centred on zero with 95% it between −2 and +2. The rejection region (rust) is the outer 5% of that distribution, meaning that D's so
far from zero that chance produces them only one time in twenty. The critical values are about ±1.9 pp. The p-value is the
share of shuffled D's at least as extreme as the actual one. −0.97 sits inside the grayish brown region, and we do not reject H₀ at any significance level. Switch the dropdown to "expected
inflation rose most", and we get D = +1.55 pp. This is the
closest any version comes to the rejection region, and at 10% one-sided it just clears it. The 12-month-inflation version
(p = 0.79) and the "expectations fell" version (p = 0.21) do not. 
The interval (Fig. 1), the null (Fig. 3) and the actual D all point at the
same thing. That is, we cannot reject the null hypothesis. 

<iframe src="fig3_null.html" width="100%" height="560" style="border:none;" loading="lazy" title="Figure 3: D under the null, rejection region and p-value"></iframe>

*Figure 3. Distribution of D when the labels are shuffled at random for 10000 times; the rust bins are the two-sided
rejection region at the chosen significance level, the vertical line the actual D.*

## Caveats

- The shuffle and the resample could ignore that volatility
clusters in time, so a block bootstrap would widen the intervals instead of
narrowing them. This might be the reason why non-rejection survives.
- The CPI
for month t is published in the middle of month t+1, so "the month inflation was highest" is known only after gold's return
for that month. On the other hand, the breakeven version has no such lag, which is one reason to trust it more. 

## Takeaway

To conclude, looking at the entire history, gold is not a good hedge against inflation.
It gained about 0.08% in the 29 highest-inflation months against 1.05% in ordinary ones. 
A better characterization of gold is that gold responds to a *change in expected* inflation.
When the breakeven rose most, D = +1.55 and p = 0.11, and the
10-year breakeven has sat between 2.15% and 2.46% for the last 30 months — stable expectations, nothing to react to. So
"gold as an inflation hedge" is a bet on expectations moving, not on prices rising. Label any broader claim as a hypothesis
and point to MS and to Post 7 (gold's real-yield and dollar exposures). And the methodological line: a 95% interval from
five years of data covered the truth 88% of the time — short histories overstate their own precision.]

## Data and code

- Gold: COMEX gold futures, front-month continuous contract (`GC=F`), monthly total return from month-end closes, from Yahoo Finance daily data via `yfinance`
- Inflation print: Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers, all items, seasonally adjusted (`CPIAUCSL`, Bureau of
  Labor Statistics), monthly, via FRED
- Expected inflation: 10-year breakeven inflation rate (`T10YIE`, Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis), month-end value via
  FRED

Code, data and every number quoted above: [github.com/1iiiiii/Gold-Hedge-Inference](https://github.com/1iiiiii/Gold-Hedge-Inference).
